ubiqedge — implementation spec (draft)

stack: NestJS + TypeScript + MySQL (backend, ingestion-service), React + JavaScript (frontend — plain JS, deliberately no TS on this side)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. repo layout — three deployable services + two device simulators, one shared MySQL

  /frontend             React + JavaScript (plain JS — TS would be overkill
                        for a prototype frontend of this size; backend/
                        ingestion-service keep TS since their schema-heavy,
                        FK-laden logic benefits more from static types)
  /backend              NestJS — everything except device telemetry ingestion
  /ingestion-service     NestJS — the /ingest/v1 write path only
  /meter-simulator       standalone script — mocks a physical water meter,
                         POSTs TOTAL + FLOW readings on a timer
  /tank-simulator        standalone script — mocks a level-sensor tank,
                         POSTs LEVEL readings on a timer

  meter-simulator and tank-simulator exist purely so the ingestion pipeline
  is demonstrably testable end-to-end locally without real hardware — each
  is a small Node/TS script (not a NestJS app, no framework overhead needed)
  that reads a serialNo/orgCode/interval from env/config and periodically
  calls POST /ingest/v1/:orgCode/:deviceType/:serialNo. Two separate
  directories rather than one parameterized script, since the two device
  types have different param shapes (TOTAL+FLOW vs LEVEL) and this keeps
  each trivially readable on its own.

  why split ingestion out: it's the one endpoint with a fundamentally
  different load/scaling profile (high-frequency, unauthenticated-by-JWT,
  no business logic beyond validate+write) — isolating it means it can be
  scaled or hardened independently of the CRUD backend, and a bad ingestion
  spike can't degrade the admin/customer-facing API. Both services connect to
  the same MySQL instance/schema with their own TypeORM DataSource; only
  `backend` owns/runs migrations, `ingestion-service` just reads/writes
  through a minimal set of entities for the tables it touches (device,
  device_type, device_type_param, device_telemetry, organization) —
  duplicated entity definitions, not a shared package, to keep the two
  services independently deployable without a monorepo build step. Documented
  as a deliberate tradeoff in the README (a shared `libs/entities` package
  would remove the duplication if this grew past assignment scope).


0a. scaling & reliability posture for ingestion-service

  load here is steady, not spiky (fixed device count * one call per 5 min —
  even 300+ meters is only ~1 req/sec average), so the design goal is
  reliability/HA, not raw throughput. Two properties make horizontal
  replication behind an LB safe without extra coordination:
    - stateless: no session/in-memory state in ingestion-service
    - idempotent writes: the device_telemetry unique constraint absorbs
      retried deliveries, so a request retried against a different replica
      after failover can't double-count a reading (which would corrupt billing)
  given that, replicas are sized for availability (2+ instances, so one can
  be down/deploying without losing the write path), not for load. The LB
  health check must actually verify DB connectivity (not just process
  liveness), so a replica that's lost its MySQL connection gets pulled out
  of rotation instead of silently dropping telemetry. At larger scale, MySQL
  itself — not ingestion-service — is the real single point of failure, so
  that's where primary/replica DB redundancy belongs; connection pool size
  per instance must stay bounded so replica count * pool size can't exceed
  MySQL's max_connections. Full hardware/software sizing per FR's 50/300/300+
  meter tiers goes in the separate infra-recommendation doc (deliverable #2).


1. backend — module breakdown ( maps 1:1 to api design resources )

  ( module names are singular — UserModule not UsersModule, etc. —
    TelemetryModule/PricingModule stay as-is since those nouns are already
    singular/uncountable )

  AuthModule            login, JWT issuance (passport-jwt), bcrypt password hashing
  UserModule            user CRUD
  AccountModule         customer_connection CRUD, status (ACTIVE/SUSPENDED)
  DeviceModule          device + device_type + device_type_param CRUD
  TelemetryModule       telemetry query only (consumption view) — GET /devices/:id/telemetry
  PricingModule         pricing_config + pricing_slab CRUD, effective-config resolution
  InvoiceModule         admin-triggered batch generation service, invoice CRUD
  PaymentModule         payment session init, /payments/v1/webhook ingress, SignatureGuard
  OrganizationModule    org bootstrap/lookup (mostly seed-driven, thin)
  CommandModule         STRETCH — device_command (relay cutoff)
  WebhookModule         STRETCH — outbound webhook_subscription dispatch

  cross-cutting:
    - CommonModule: global ValidationPipe, global exception filter, RolesGuard,
      @Roles() decorator, @CurrentUser() decorator (pulls orgId/userId/role off JWT)
    - every service method that reads/writes accepts the authenticated user's
      orgId implicitly (from @CurrentUser()) — never from request body/params —
      this is what actually enforces multi-tenant isolation, the guards alone don't.


1a. API versioning — NestJS built-in versioning module, not hand-prefixed paths

  - backend: app.setGlobalPrefix('api', { exclude: ['payments/(.*)'] }), then
    app.enableVersioning({ type: VersioningType.URI, defaultVersion: '1' }).
    Controllers just declare @Controller('users') etc — Nest resolves the
    final route to /api/v1/users. PaymentsWebhookController is excluded from
    the global prefix and declares its own @Controller({ path: 'payments',
    version: '1' }) → /payments/v1/webhook/:provider
  - ingestion-service: app.setGlobalPrefix('ingest'); same
    app.enableVersioning({ type: VersioningType.URI, defaultVersion: '1' })
    → /ingest/v1/:orgCode/:deviceType/:serialNo
  - benefit over manually prefixing every path with '/v1': a future breaking
    change (e.g. ingestion payload shape) becomes additive —
    @Controller({ version: '2' }) on a new controller, old routes untouched —
    instead of a find-replace across the codebase


2. ORM & migrations

  TypeORM, chosen over Prisma for this project because:
    - migrations are plain SQL-generatable from entities (`typeorm migration:generate`),
      useful to hand-review given the FK-heavy schema
    - QueryRunner gives explicit transaction control needed for invoice batch
      generation and the payment-webhook write (insert payment_transaction +
      update invoice.status atomically)

  entities mirror ubiqedge_tech_data_model 1:1. @DeleteDateColumn() on:
    user, customer_connection, device
  (customer_invoice and payment_transaction are NOT soft-deletable — financial
  records use the CANCELLED status instead, to preserve the audit trail)

  all FK relations use { onDelete: 'RESTRICT' } explicitly — soft delete means
  a hard DELETE should never silently cascade or null out a still-referenced
  row; RESTRICT forces it to fail loudly instead.

  migration workflow (adapted from the team's TypeORM migration practice):
    - src/database/data-source.ts — standalone DataSource for the CLI, glob
      entity discovery (`**/*.entity.ts`), separate from the Nest runtime's
      TypeOrmModule.forRootAsync (which uses autoLoadEntities instead)
    - synchronize is always false — migrations are the only source of truth
      for schema changes, never auto-sync
    - npm scripts: migration:show / migration:run / migration:revert /
      migration:generate — all build first (`nest build`), then run the
      typeorm CLI against compiled dist/database/data-source.js
    - migration:generate produces one big diff file first (not committed —
      it's a throwaway blueprint), which then gets split into small,
      table-specific migration files (one CREATE TABLE per file, including
      that table's own FK constraints inline, ordered by dependency) rather
      than committing one giant migration
    - each table-specific migration was validated with a full
      run -> revert (all the way to empty) -> run cycle against a live MySQL
      container before being treated as final, and the resulting schema was
      diffed against ubiqedge_tech_data_model via SHOW CREATE TABLE — not
      just assumed correct from the entity code


3. auth

  - passport-jwt strategy, access token only (no refresh token — out of scope
    for this assignment's size; short-ish expiry, e.g. 8h, documented as a
    known limitation)
  - JwtAuthGuard + RolesGuard, applied globally via APP_GUARD in `backend`,
    opted out with @Public() where needed (login, payment webhook)
  - SignatureGuard (payment webhook, in `backend`): verifies provider's HMAC
    signature header against the raw request body before it reaches the
    controller
  - ApiKeyGuard lives in `ingestion-service`, not backend (see section 0):
    looks up the org by :orgCode, compares X-Api-Key against a hashed secret
    stored on organization (add `apiKeySecretHash` column — small addition to
    the organization table). It's the only guard that service needs — no JWT
    strategy in ingestion-service at all.


4. invoice generation — admin-triggered, not a background job

  - per FR: "Admin can generate invoices for all meters for previous month" —
    this is an explicit admin action (POST /invoices/generate), not an
    automatic monthly schedule. No @nestjs/schedule / cron in this design;
    dropped it entirely rather than keep an unused dependency around.
  - InvoicesService.generateForPeriod(orgId, period) is the single entry
    point the controller calls — kept as its own service method (not inlined
    in the controller) so it's independently unit-testable, not because
    anything else invokes it
  - defaults to the previous calendar month if the admin doesn't specify a
    period, but accepts an explicit period param for admin-driven backfills/reruns
  - per device, inside one QueryRunner transaction:
      1. resolve opening checkpoint (see ubiqedge_tech_data_model note on the
         anchor query — last non-CANCELLED invoice, or earliest telemetry)
      2. resolve closing checkpoint (latest TOTAL reading <= period end)
      3. resolve active pricing_config for the device's deviceTypeId
      4. compute consumptionUnits, appliedUnitRate, amount
           - FIXED: amount = consumptionUnits * fixedRate
           - SLAB: walk pricing_slab tiers, sum tier-bounded consumption * tier
             rate; appliedUnitRate stored = amount / consumptionUnits (blended,
             per the earlier decision — no line-item breakdown table)
      5. insert customer_invoice row
  - failures for one device (no telemetry that period, no active pricing
    config, etc.) are caught, logged with deviceId + reason, and skipped —
    they must NOT abort the whole batch or leave a partial row
  - re-running for an already-invoiced period is a no-op per device (unique
    constraint), so the admin-triggered endpoint is safe to call repeatedly


5. validation & error handling

  - class-validator DTOs per resource (CreateXDto/UpdateXDto), global
    ValidationPipe({ whitelist: true, forbidNonWhitelisted: true, transform: true })
  - global exception filter normalizes all errors (including TypeORM
    QueryFailedError, e.g. duplicate-key) to { statusCode, message, error,
    path, timestamp }; duplicate-key on device_telemetry ingest is treated as
    a success (idempotent retry), not surfaced as a client error
  - structured logging (nestjs-pino or built-in Logger with JSON output) —
    every ingestion request and invoice batch run logs orgId/deviceId context,
    since these are the two flows most likely to need after-the-fact debugging


6. testing

  - unit tests (Jest) only, for now: pricing calculation (FIXED + SLAB,
    including tier boundaries), opening-checkpoint anchor resolution (incl.
    the gap/CANCELLED cases), invoice idempotency — these are the parts with
    real logic worth protecting
  - e2e/integration tests (supertest) deferred — out of scope for now given
    time budget, not a correctness gap in the design. Call this out under
    "what I'd improve with more time" in the README rather than skipping it
    silently.


7. local runnability

  docker-compose.yml:
    - mysql:8 service
    - backend service (NestJS, runs migrations on boot, then a seed script)
    - ingestion-service (NestJS, no migrations of its own — starts after
      backend's migrations have run, since it depends on the schema existing)
    - frontend service (React, served via vite preview or nginx)

  seed script creates: one organization, Admin + Customer roles, device_types
  (METER billed=true, TANK billed=false) with their params, one default
  pricing_config (FIXED), one admin user — the minimum needed to log in and
  start creating devices/accounts through the API/UI.

