8. frontend — React + JavaScript + Cloudscape

  - plain JavaScript, not TypeScript — TS would be overkill for a prototype
    UI of this size; the type-safety payoff matters far more on the
    backend/ingestion-service side (FK-laden entities, migration-generated
    schema) than on a handful of pages calling a handful of endpoints
  - UI: Cloudscape Design System components throughout, not a bespoke/ad hoc
    component set
  - state: Redux Toolkit slices (not TanStack Query — superseded decision).
    Server data lives in slices too; loading/error/data state per resource
  - layered call path, one direction only: Component -> custom hook (e.g.
    useDevices(), useInvoices()) -> a single API client wrapper -> backend.
    Components never call the wrapper directly, and the wrapper never
    contains resource-specific logic — it's the one place that knows about
    base URL, auth header injection, and error normalization. This
    single-abstraction-point layering is deliberate for evolvability: the
    wrapper can change transport (fetch vs axios) or add retry/interceptor
    behavior without touching hooks or components, and a hook can change how
    it uses Redux (e.g. add caching/memoization) without touching components
  - React Router, role-based route guards reading the decoded JWT (redirect
    to /login on 401 — handled centrally in the API wrapper, not per-hook)
  - react-hook-form + zod for forms, schemas kept close to the backend DTOs
    so client-side and server-side validation stay in sync
  - list-screen search inputs are debounced client-side before calling the
    `search` query param the backend already supports (Invoice list now,
    User/Device lists once retrofitted — see task tracking `search`)
  - token storage: localStorage (reversed from an original in-memory-only
    design). The token is readable by any injected script for as long as
    it sits there (up to JWT_EXPIRES_IN, currently 8h) — accepted
    explicitly so the session survives a page refresh, a usability need
    that outweighed the XSS-surface tradeoff for this prototype's scope.
    Call this out in the README as a known limitation: the properly secure
    fix is an httpOnly cookie + backend refresh-token support, still out of
    scope (see section 3)

  - role-based visibility is enforced at the page/nav level, not just via
    route guards redirecting after the fact: which pages exist in
    navigation and which routes are even reachable is derived from the
    decoded JWT's roleType at login — a Customer session must never render
    Admin pages/nav items (and vice versa), not just block the API calls
    they'd make

  routes/pages:
    /login
    Admin:
      /admin/users            list/create/edit
      /admin/accounts         list/create, link user
      /admin/devices          list/create, assign to account, unassigned filter
      /admin/devices/:id      detail + telemetry chart (time-window picker)
      /admin/pricing          create/view FIXED or SLAB configs
      /admin/invoices         list, filter by period/status, trigger generation
      /admin/invoices/:id     detail, cancel action
    Customer:
      /account                own account + linked devices
      /account/devices/:id    consumption view — chart + time-window picker (req #4)
      /invoices               current + previous invoices (req #5)
      /invoices/:id           detail (+ pay action, appreciated #2)

  - consumption view uses a simple line/bar chart (recharts) over
    GET /devices/:id/telemetry?from=&to=&paramKey= — this is the one place a
    chart earns its place; everything else is tables/forms

