9. build priority ( this is an interview assignment — reviewers weigh judgment
   and clarity as much as feature count, so sequence accordingly )

  1. required functionality end-to-end, working and testable locally:
     auth, user/device CRUD, ingestion, consumption view, invoice generation
  2. README: run instructions, schema explanation, design decisions +
     assumptions, what you'd improve — this is an explicit deliverable, not
     an afterthought
  3. unit tests around the logic that's actually tricky (pricing, checkpoint
     anchoring, idempotency) — demonstrates rigor without burning time on
     exhaustive coverage
  4. appreciated/stretch items (payment gateway, multiple ingestion
     mechanisms, webhook egress, supply cutoff, e2e tests) — genuinely
     optional, pick up only what's left after 1–3 are solid, and it's fine to
     list the rest under "what I'd improve with more time" instead of
     building them


10. open items / assumptions to note in the README

  - FR doesn't explicitly require a frontend UI — building one anyway for a
    usable "view consumption / view invoices" experience; document this as a
    deliberate scope addition, not a requirement reinterpretation
  - no refresh-token flow — access-token-only JWT, session length is a
    documented tradeoff
  - no forgot-password flow — a real implementation needs a way to prove
    identity without the password, which almost always means emailing a
    reset link/token, and this stack has no email-sending capability at all
    (unlike payments, which got a mock gateway rather than being skipped —
    forgot-password was judged lower priority for this assignment's scope
    and left out entirely rather than half-built). Admin can still create/
    manage all users, so there's no account-lockout dead end, just no
    self-service reset
  - slab pricing stores a blended appliedUnitRate on the invoice, not a
    line-item-per-tier breakdown (decided earlier — simpler, still auditable
    via pricingConfigId)
  - device_command / webhook_subscription (stretch tables) only get built if
    core scope + tests are solid first
